## Root Cause
The test does:

```ts
test('home page greets with Hello world', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/');
  await expect(page.locator('h1')).toHaveText('Hello world');
});
```

`tests/playwright.config.ts` boots the app via its `webServer` entry:
`docker compose up --build frontend` with `cwd: '..'`, waiting on
`http://127.0.0.1:${FRONTEND_PORT}`. `docker-compose.yaml` builds that service
from `context: ./services/frontend` and maps `${FRONTEND_PORT}:3000`, so the app
must listen on port **3000 inside the container** and bind `0.0.0.0`.

But `services/frontend/` does not exist at all (there is no `services/`
directory in the repo). The compose build therefore fails, no server ever comes
up, Playwright's `webServer` wait times out and the test fails.

The fix is to create the minimal Next.js (App Router) frontend service that
`docs/architecture/architecture.yaml` declares (`services/frontend`,
typescript + next.js + react, quality gate config at
`services/frontend/tsconfig.json`) and that renders an `<h1>` whose text is
exactly `Hello world` at `/`.

